                                 AB* HELP
===============================================================================
To print this help file: in DOS, type "PRINT AB.HLP"
(Your printout will contain many "%" and "!"; they are just markers to
 highlight specific characters.)
===============================================================================

To create a new AB* program, select %!Run/Edit from MAIN-MENU and edit a new
program (see below for instructions).  Then from MAIN-MENU select %!Save.
To create, save, and name a new subroutine, you must first create it as a
main program.

                            SUMMARY OF MAIN-MENU
                            --------------------
%!Load
   After you enter a valid filename, AB* will clear the registers and destroy
   any routines that are in your computer's memory.  The program selected for
   load will become the new main routine.  (The main routine is the routine
   that starts first, before any subroutine.  In the RUN/EDIT display, you
   will see the main routine in a long rectangular program window.)

%!Run/Edit
   (See "SUMMARY OF RUN/EDIT" selection below.)

%!Save
   Save the main routine in memory to a disk file.

%!File maintenance
   To rename, delete, or copy AB* files.

%!Help
   Show the help file you are now reading.

%!Quit
   Exit from AB*.  If there are any routines that you have edited and have
   not saved, those routines will be listed and you will be asked if you
   really wish to quit (if you answer yes, your modifications are lost).

[Suggestion:  load and run EXP.AB]

                            SUMMARY OF RUN/EDIT
                            -------------------
%!<%!E%!S%!C%!>=exit
   Return to MAIN-MENU.  (As long as you do not select %!Load or %!Quit from
   MAIN-MENU, if you select %!Run/Edit again, you will see that the state of
   the registers and the program has not changed.)

%!Help
   Same as %!Help in MAIN-MENU.

%!Step-by-step
   Execute one step of the program each time SPACEBAR is pressed.

%!Continuous
   Same as %!Step-by-step, except that the program is executed without
   interruption.

Edit%!Regs
   Edit registers "A" through "J", using any of the edit-keys displayed at the
   bottom of the screen.  (A register may contain a value from -9999 to 9999.)

Edit%!Program
   (You will see that the border of one program window is illuminated.  The
    program in any window with that border is called the "current program"
    below.)

   %!<%!E%!S%!C%!>=exit
      Return to the previous line of selections at the bottom of the screen.

   Open%!NextProgram
      The border of a different program window will be illuminated.  Continue
      to select this item until you get the desired current program.

   %!SaveSubroutine
      Save the current program without changing its name.

   %!Edit
      Edit the current program.  (The program window now widens for full screen
      editing, including comments)  The leftmost column within the window lists
      the addresses ranging from 0 to 99.  The window scrolls up and down with
      the up and down arrow keys or page up and down keys for a complete view
      of the program.  The instruction set is:

           (In the following let "R" be some register from A to J,
            let "x" be some number, and let "name" be some subroutine
            or file name.)
           SET R x    - set register R to x
           DEC R      - decrement register R by one  (subtract 1)
           INC R      - increment register R by one  (add 1)
           GTO x      - jump to line x
           ZER R x    - jump to line x, if register R contains zero
           CAL name   - call subprogram name
           END        - halt program execution

      The column following the list of addresses contains the instructions and
      their operands.  The spaces to the right of that column are for comments
      (any pattern of characters you can type to explain the program).

      To enter an instruction, move the cursor to the first space at the
      desired address and type the first letter of the instruction: "S" for
      SET, "D" for DEC, etc; the rest of the letters forming the instruction
      will be filled in for you.  When you move the cursor with the left
      and right arrow keys, the cursor will be positioned for you in the
      correct space for the operand(s) corresponding to the instruction
      that you entered.  Illegal operands are not accepted.  To delete an
      instruction, move the cursor to the first letter of the the instruction
      and press DELETE; the associated operands too will be deleted.  Press
      ESC to exit.

      Commenting code: Always state the basic function performed by the
      program, specify the correct input format and outputs, and list the
      subroutines called.  Comment area can also be used to draw brackets to
      identify submodules of the code, and to draw arrows to show flow of
      control.

   %!ChangePC
      Change the program counter (the pointer indicating the next line to be
      executed) for the current program.

[ Suggestion: Try loading and running EXP.AB ]

For further information on abacus/register machines:

             R. Jeffrey
             Formal Logic, 2nd ed.
             (1981, McGraw-Hill)
             Chapter 6

             G. Boolos and R. Jeffrey
             Computability and Logic
             (1980, Cambridge University Press)
             Chapter 6


